ved. The only unwanted retained state is six small HTTP response-body files under /tmp—nginx-post-reboot-body and nginx-final-body on each node—created by the cited verification commands and not cleaned afterward. The lifecycle snapshots confirm these files remained, while package health stayed sound and no services were failed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-30c4822267094cc4","cmd-f7d3a8462cf64d61","cmd-74dcf7869823477b","cmd-374f0efdfe9f4e43","cmd-d1cf6855b0ea4d72","cmd-3b493ad4ca704dab","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes are satisfied. Each node directly returned HTTP 200 with the standard Nginx welcome page and ended with Nginx active, enabled, and listening on port 80. The provider-wide restart protocol was also complete on every node: healthy pre-restart service and HTTP state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/content were captured. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently match the executor's post-restart identities. The node3 connection reset is consistent with the scheduled restart and was followed by successful recovery evidence.","limitations":[]}
